<%# The mission page, adapted from docs/VISION.md for a reader who has never
    heard the argument and did not come here for a manifesto. Concrete
    before abstract: the thing you don't have, then why you don't have it. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Think about what a wealthy person actually buys. Not the car — the
    people. Someone who keeps the calendar and books the appointment.
    Someone who reads the letter from the insurer and knows it can be
    appealed. Someone who does the taxes, checks the lease, tutors the kid,
    and remembers that the renewal is in March.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of that is genius. Almost all of it is attention, applied steadily
    to a life by someone who has read the paperwork. It is the most
    boringly practical advantage money buys, and it compounds — because the
    deposit gets recovered, the denial gets appealed, the raise gets asked
    for, and the deadline doesn't pass.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the first time, software can do a real share of that work. The
    question is who ends up owning it.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Rent, or own</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The obvious path is the one already happening: assistance arrives as a
    subscription from a handful of very large companies. You type your life
    into their servers; they read it; you pay monthly; and if they change
    the terms, raise the price, or switch the product off, that is simply
    what happens next. This has a name in every other market. It's tenancy.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    We think that's a bad ending, and not mainly for privacy reasons. If
    the productivity gain from this technology is only ever available as
    rent, then the gain concentrates at the top of the lease — permanently,
    structurally, in a way no amount of goodwill from the landlord fixes.
    The people who most need an assistant, an accountant and an advocate
    are exactly the people who will be priced into the cheap tier of one.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Why software, and not a policy</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Rules can be repealed by whoever wins the next election. Companies get
    acquired and shut things off. But software that runs on hardware people
    already own, that copies for free, and that nobody has the authority to
    switch off, is very hard to take back. That pattern has worked before:
    Linux, Signal, BitTorrent. One person can write a thing that millions
    end up running, and after that it is simply out there.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    So the counter-move isn't to lobby for a better landlord. It's to lower
    the cost of ownership until an ordinary household can have the whole
    stack on a computer in the spare room.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What follows from that</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The memory is the asset") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        What makes an assistant useful is not the model — it's everything it
        knows about you. That knowledge is the part worth owning, and here
        it is a file on your disk. The model is a component you can swap out
        on a Tuesday.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Built for the 99%") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Not for developers, not for enterprises. For people who have never
        had an assistant, an accountant or a lawyer, and who are the reason
        this is worth building at all. If a feature only makes sense to
        someone technical, it's a bug.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Useful before it's clever") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The first job is to be a good place to keep a life — calendar,
        documents, tasks, people. That is worth having with the intelligence
        switched off entirely, and everything cleverer is built on top of it
        rather than in place of it.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Adoption beats elegance") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A beautiful tool nobody can install protects nobody. Every decision
        that makes this easier to get running is a mission decision, not a
        convenience one.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The honest part</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    This is not a finished answer. Running your own software is still harder
    than signing up for something, the models you can run at home are behind
    the ones you can rent, and a house full of self-hosted services is not
    everybody's idea of a good evening. All of that is true and we'd rather
    say so than pretend.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    What we'd argue is that the gap is closing from both directions, and
    that the thing worth building now is the part that doesn't close by
    itself: somewhere for a household's life to live that belongs to the
    household.
  </p>
</section>
